ng and supports on these  buildings and sometimes also some  thatched roofs which I was actually  looking into recently are apparently  called makuu tea I am probably  mispronouncing literally everything I'm  saying in this video but yeah it's just  a really cool simple style that doesn't  take too many items if you're looking at  planets do for instance and how you  could build it in this game but it very  quickly comes together in a really  beautiful picture it's also really cool  because you can play a lot with depth  because the the textures and detailing  is so simple and pretty laid back that  really the interesting thing to play  with is composition and depth in some of  your buildings I think so that's also  what I'm doing here a lot you barely  find any straight pieces or boxy parts  of this building everything's kind of  set back from each other or overhanging  something else and placed at a slightly  different angle which altogether creates  this really interesting sort of Rhapsody  of simple parts of the building I've  also found that a lot of these buildings  tend to have  vertical but also horizontal support  beams made out of mangrove wood which I  wanted to bring back into this building  as well with the porch for instance  which is raised on the left side there  kind of like a trellis almost and of  course all of the little windows as well  now I'm making this building out of the  the plaster pieces because they're just  so versatile I still love them so much  and in that sense the way that I'm  working this is kind of inspired by  Teleco which is an old planet coaster  project bifida bear and at foe TV which  isn't even finished as I'm speaking it's  something that's been going for years  but it uses very similar architecture  and back then they used Windows that the  backside of windows in planet coaster to  make these walls obviously we have the  plaster pieces nowadays which is much  easier and much less heavy on your  computer but the same kind